<p>David Charles, professor emeritus of philosophy at Yale University, has died.<span id="more-58074"></span></p>
<p><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-58075 aligncenter" src="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/charles-david-philosopher.png" alt="" width="360" height="360" srcset="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/charles-david-philosopher.png 360w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/charles-david-philosopher-300x300.png 300w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/charles-david-philosopher-150x150.png 150w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/charles-david-philosopher-177x177.png 177w" sizes="(max-width: 360px) 100vw, 360px" /></p>
<p>Professor Charles was well-known for his work in ancient philosophy, and especially on Aristotle. He is the author of <em>The Undivided Self </em>(2021), <em>Aristotle on Meaning and Essence </em>(2000), <em>Aristotle’s Philosophy of Action</em> (1984), among other works, which you can learn more about <a href="https://philpeople.org/profiles/david-charles" target="_blank" rel="noopener">here</a>.</p>
<p>Professor Charles joined the faculty at Yale in 2014. Before that, he was at the University of Oxford. After retiring from Yale he was a visiting professor at Stanford University. He earned his D.Phil. from Oxford.</p>
<p>He died on August 12th.</p>
